Description: Read every template through one hook per tokenizer state
 A template range may carry its kind (print, tag or comment). The tokenizer
 hands a template character to the state's parseTemplate hook instead of
 parse, so parse only ever sees HTML characters and no string compare can
 match template text. A state without a hook takes the template as content.
 The rule the hooks apply: a print continues what it touches, a Twig tag
 stands alone, a Twig comment is nothing, and inside content all three are
 content. So "<" followed by a print opens a tag; any template glued to a
 tag name stays in the open tag start; among the attributes a tag is an
 attribute of its own and a comment is skipped; a print continues an
 attribute name, and any template starts an unquoted value. The doctype
 states keep upstream's reading. Templates in script, style and raw text are
 content, so a Twig string holding "</script>" cannot end the element.
Author: Numiko
Origin: vendor
Forwarded: no
Test: test/parser/parse-for-eslint.test.ts ("Twig tags and verbatim content inside markup", "Twig as part of the markup structure"); npm run test:upstream
Last-Update: 2026-11-04
